    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-86124",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a78860ea-192f-5df9-aca9-4f67ec6ffefe",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating a Rational Function Given Part Of the Partial Fraction Decomposition",
      "problem": "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{D}{x+2},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the constant $D.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4x+3 & =2x(x+2)+D({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2)^{2}+4(-2)+3 & =2(-2)(-2+2)+D((-2)^{2}+3) \\\\ 7 & =7D \\\\ D & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+3=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+3| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
