      "math_academy_id": "q-3614",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3c22b8e9-049a-5574-86c9-35788c410421",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating a Rational Function With an Irreducible Binomial Quadratic Factor",
      "problem": "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+12}{(x-2)({x}^{2}+4)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+4$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+12}{(x-2)({x}^{2}+4)}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+4}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)({x}^{2}+4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x+12=A({x}^{2}+4)+(Bx+C)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=3,B=-2,$ and $C=0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+12}{(x-2)({x}^{2}+4)} & =\\frac{3}{x-2}+\\frac{-2x+0}{{x}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x-2}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+12}{(x-2)({x}^{2}+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x-2}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|.\n$$\n\n             \n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+4.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+12}{(x-2)({x}^{2}+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$",
