      "knowledge_point": "Integrating a Rational Function With an Irreducible Trinomial Quadratic Factor",
      "problem": "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD=(-6)^{2}-4(2)(9)=-36<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{C}{x-4}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n15-25x=(Ax+B)(x-4)+C(2{x}^{2}-6x+9).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=10,B=-15$ and $C=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}=\\frac{10x-15}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{-5}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10x-15}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{-5}{x-4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u=2{x}^{2}-6x+9.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(4x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}-6x+9| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9))-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$",
